        "text_aKgTLH": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<h1><strong>The Science of Forest Medicine</strong></h1><p><em>Reviewed by </em>Brian Nichol, MD, D.ABA<em>, Medical Director</em></p><h2><strong>Where it begins</strong></h2><p>In 1982, Tomohide Akiyama, then head of Japan's Forestry Agency, gave a name to something people had always done. He called it <em>Shinrin-Yoku</em>, forest bathing: going into the woods, slowing down, and taking in the atmosphere of the forest through all the senses. It began as a public invitation to spend time among the country's trees. It became a research field.</p><p>Over the four decades since, scientists in Japan, Korea, China, Europe, and North America have followed people into forests with blood pressure cuffs, saliva kits, and heart-rate monitors, and compared what they found with the same people in city streets. The body of work is now large enough to have its own systematic reviews and its own name in the medical literature: Forest Medicine.</p><h2><strong>What forest air contains</strong></h2><p>Forest air is not empty. Trees and understory plants release aromatic compounds into the air around them, and they release a great deal: plants worldwide emit on the order of a billion tons of these volatile compounds every year. The blue haze that gives the Great Smoky Mountains their name is made in part by them.</p><p>The Russian biologist Boris Tokin gave these plant emissions a name in the late 1920s: <em>phytoncides</em>. Chemically, most belong to a family called terpenes. Alpha-pinene and beta-pinene come from pines and other conifers. Limonene comes from citrus and many evergreens. Others come from cedar, cypress, and the flowering plants of the forest floor. Together they are what a forest smells like.</p><p>The mix is never the same twice. A 2020 review of the research on forest air found that its composition changes with the species of tree, the season, the temperature, and the time of day, with concentrations generally highest in warm months (Antonelli et al., 2020). City air carries far less of these plant compounds and more of the compounds produced by traffic and industry.</p><h2><strong>How we take it in</strong></h2><p>People meet the forest's chemistry mainly through the sense of smell, and smell works by two routes. The first is the familiar one: air drawn in through the nose. The second is less well known. When something aromatic is in the mouth and you breathe out, its aroma travels up the back of the throat to the same sensory tissue high in the nose. Scientists call this <em>retronasal olfaction</em>. The psychologist Paul Rozin described the two routes in 1982, and later work showed that most of what people call flavor is in fact aroma arriving by the second route (Rozin, 1982; Shepherd, 2006). The tongue itself detects only sweet, salty, sour, bitter, and umami.</p><p>Humans have roughly four hundred types of smell receptor, and the nerve pathway from the nose runs more directly to the brain regions involved in memory and emotion than the pathways for sight or hearing do. This is the anatomical reason a single smell can bring back a place or a person in an instant.</p><h2><strong>What the field studies measured</strong></h2><p>The best-known study in the field was published in 2010 by Bum Jin Park and colleagues. They ran the same experiment in 24 forests across Japan with 280 participants in total. Each person spent time walking in and viewing a forest on one day and a city area on another. In the forest settings, the researchers recorded lower salivary cortisol, lower pulse rate, lower blood pressure, and greater parasympathetic nerve activity than in the city settings (Park et al., 2010).</p><p>A second line of work, led by Dr. Qing Li of Nippon Medical School, looked at the immune system. In studies of multi-day forest trips, Li's group reported higher natural killer cell activity in participants afterward, a change that did not appear after city trips (Li, 2010).</p><p>In 2019, a systematic review and meta-analysis pooled the studies that measured cortisol. It found that cortisol levels were significantly lower in forest groups than in comparison groups (Antonelli, Barbieri, and Donelli, 2019). A broader state-of-the-art review two years earlier reached a similar overall conclusion about short-term physiological and psychological findings (Hansen, Jones, and Tocchini, 2017).</p><h2><strong>More than the air</strong></h2><p>A forest is also something to look at and listen to, and that part has its own research. In a study published in <em>Science</em> in 1984, Roger Ulrich compared hospital patients recovering from the same surgery in rooms that differed in one respect: the window looked out on trees or on a brick wall. Patients with the view of trees had shorter stays after surgery and needed fewer strong pain medications (Ulrich, 1984).</p><p>More recently, researchers have asked whether a recorded forest carries any of this. One 2020 experiment compared a few minutes in a real outdoor forest setting, a 360-degree virtual-reality video of the same setting, and an indoor room with no nature. Participants rated both the real and the virtual forest as more restorative than the indoor room, and the real forest did the most for positive mood (Browning et al., 2020).</p><h2><strong>Why it matters now</strong></h2><p>For nearly all of human history, people lived among plants. That changed quickly. According to the United Nations, 55 percent of the world's population lived in urban areas in 2018, and the share is projected to reach 68 percent by 2050. Much of the interest in Forest Medicine comes from this shift, and from a simple question it raises: what, if anything, do people lose when the forest is no longer part of daily life?</p><h2><strong>What is not yet settled</strong></h2><p>The research is promising and it is also young. The reviews that summarize it are candid about its limits:</p><ul><li>Many studies are small, and many involve healthy young adults, most often men.</li><li>Most measure short-term changes over hours or days. Long-term effects are much less studied.</li><li>A forest visit bundles many things together: the air, the quiet, the light, walking, and time away from work. Separating the contribution of each is difficult. Small laboratory studies have begun to test whether tree-derived compounds alone account for part of the findings (Li et al., 2009), but that question is far from closed.</li><li>Expectation plays a role. The 2019 cortisol meta-analysis noted that simply anticipating a forest visit appeared to influence cortisol, and that the quality of the available studies was mixed.</li></ul><p>The fair summary is that being in a forest is consistently associated with measurable short-term changes in the body, and that scientists are still working out why, for whom, and for how long.</p><h2><strong>Where BlissThera® fits</strong></h2><p>BlissThera® practices Forest Medicine in its clinics in Little Rock, Arkansas, using its own patented technology, and it makes its own forest botanical formulas. Our work starts from the research described here. You can read about the clinical practice at <a href=\"https://www.blissthera.com\"><span style=\"text-decoration:underline\">blissthera.com</span></a>.</p><p>Healthcare professionals who would like technical material about our clinical practice may contact our Medical Director through [contact page or email].</p><h2><strong>Sources</strong></h2><ul><li>Antonelli M, Barbieri G, Donelli D. Effects of forest bathing (shinrin-yoku) on levels of cortisol as a stress biomarker: a systematic review and meta-analysis. <em>International Journal of Biometeorology.</em> 2019;63(8):1117-1134.</li><li>Antonelli M, Donelli D, Barbieri G, Valussi M, Maggini V, Firenzuoli F. Forest volatile organic compounds and their effects on human health: a state-of-the-art review. <em>International Journal of Environmental Research and Public Health.</em> 2020;17(18):6506.</li><li>Browning MHEM, Mimnaugh KJ, van Riper CJ, Laurent HK, LaValle SM. Can simulated nature support mental health? Comparing short, single-doses of 360-degree nature videos in virtual reality with the outdoors. <em>Frontiers in Psychology.</em> 2020;10:2667.</li><li>Hansen MM, Jones R, Tocchini K. Shinrin-yoku (forest bathing) and nature therapy: a state-of-the-art review. <em>International Journal of Environmental Research and Public Health.</em> 2017;14(8):851.</li><li>Li Q. Effect of forest bathing trips on human immune function. <em>Environmental Health and Preventive Medicine.</em> 2010;15(1):9-17.</li><li>Li Q, Kobayashi M, Wakayama Y, et al. Effect of phytoncide from trees on human natural killer cell function. <em>International Journal of Immunopathology and Pharmacology.</em> 2009;22(4):951-959.</li><li>Park BJ, Tsunetsugu Y, Kasetani T, Kagawa T, Miyazaki Y. The physiological effects of shinrin-yoku (taking in the forest atmosphere or forest bathing): evidence from field experiments in 24 forests across Japan. <em>Environmental Health and Preventive Medicine.</em> 2010;15(1):18-26.</li><li>Rozin P. \"Taste-smell confusions\" and the duality of the olfactory sense. <em>Perception and Psychophysics.</em> 1982;31(4):397-401.</li><li>Shepherd GM. Smell images and the flavour system in the human brain. <em>Nature.</em> 2006;444(7117):316-321.</li><li>Ulrich RS. View through a window may influence recovery from surgery. <em>Science.</em> 1984;224(4647):420-421.</li><li>United Nations, Department of Economic and Social Affairs. <em>World Urbanization Prospects: The 2018 Revision.</em></li></ul><p><em>This page summarizes published research about forest environments. It is provided for general education and is not medical advice. These statements have not been evaluated by the Food and Drug Administration. BlissThera® products are not intended to diagnose, treat, cure, or prevent any disease.</em></p>"
          }
        },
